{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794652457,"nonce":"6ewgNtPjk7LbBP5iUMuFwixdaBc=","message":"Requirement (user, 2026-11-14): every phase of the pipeline is zero-copy. This changes the wire format: postcard decodes into owned structures, so it is not zero-copy for the hot path.\n\nMeaning: no phase re-encodes or bulk-copies data it received. A phase either borrows its input or builds new data that refers to the old by index or offset. Four steps cannot avoid producing new data: the Haxe macro writing the bytes once, AIR construction, inlining (it duplicates code by definition), and code emission.\n\nProbe (Haxe 4.3.6, the unit suite, 298616 nodes, same tree as the postcard probe). A flat arena: a 16-byte header (magic, version, bodies, nodes), then 20-byte records of five little-endian u32 words { tag, kind, payload, first_kid, kid_count }. Nodes are written breadth-first, so a node's children are consecutive and `first_kid..first_kid + kid_count` indexes the same array; the body roots are nodes 0..bodies.\n\n    Haxe writer, fills a pre-sized Bytes with setInt32   ~645 ms (641-656), 5.97 MB\n    Rust: mmap                                           5 us\n    Rust: bounds pass over every child range             0.4 ms warm (7.9 ms cold)\n    Rust: walk all nodes from the roots                  1.6 ms\n    Allocation in Rust: none except the walk's stack. The bytes are cast in place to \u0026[NodeRec] (bytemuck).\n\nThe walk reached all 298616 nodes through the child ranges with no range out of bounds, and the tag sum (1630722) and the number of string payloads (77942) equal what the Haxe side counted. The integer-payload sum does not match: the totals differ by exactly 19 x 2^32 on every run, which points at 19 values eval holds outside 32 bits, not at the layout. I did not chase it further.\n\nThe file is about 5x the postcard one (20 bytes a node against about 4). Fixed-width fields can shrink later (u8 tags, u16 kinds), and a local mmap does not care.\n\nDecision: the Haxe-facing format is a flat arena of plain-old-data records, defined as `#[repr(C)]` Rust structs and read in place. The Haxe writer is generated from the same structs and uses setInt32 into a pre-sized buffer. Strings are an offset table into one blob. The header carries a magic number, a version and a schema hash, and the reader validates bounds once. Little-endian only; a big-endian host is refused with a clear message. Postcard drops out of the hot path.\n\nBeyond the Haxe boundary the format is Rust-to-Rust, so Haxe's limits do not apply there: the AIR disk cache and OSR should move from serde to an archived, memory-mapped layout (the flat records above or rkyv). Audit for copies: the whole-table HLType clone (memory note whole-table-clone-antipattern), Function clones between passes, and the serde round trip in air_disk_cache.rs.","files":null}]}